  "about": [
    "Guarani is spoken in Paraguay, where it has been an official language alongside Spanish since 1992 and is used by most of the population, in the countryside and in the cities. It is also spoken in northeastern Argentina (it is official in Corrientes), in Bolivia and in Brazil.",
    "It belongs to the Tupi-Guarani family. It is one of the few Indigenous languages of the Americas spoken by people of every background, not only by Indigenous peoples; in Paraguay many people mix Guarani and Spanish in what is called jopara.",
    "Its speakers call it avañe'ẽ, «the language of the people». It is written with the official alphabet, the achegety, which has 33 letters, among them six nasal vowels and the puso ('), a catch in the throat."
  ],
  "orthography": {
    "intro": "The Guarani alphabet is called achegety, after its first letters: a, che, ge. Each letter always sounds the same. The tilde (~) marks nasal vowels, and the stress is only written when it does not fall on the last syllable.",
