    "notes": [
      "y is a vowel: it is said with the tongue in the middle of the mouth, between i and u (y «water»).",
      "Stress falls on the last syllable unless it is written: ajapo, karu. If it falls elsewhere, it is marked: óga «house», ajapóta «I will do».",
      "Nasality spreads: one nasal vowel or consonant makes the whole word nasal, and the prefixes change (jajapo «we do», but ñamba'apo «we work»).",
      "The puso (') is a short catch in the throat, as in «uh-oh»: ka'a «yerba mate».",
      "r is always a single tap, as in Spanish «pero»; rr, l and a few other letters only appear in words from Spanish.",
      "Many texts write g instead of g̃ (guahẽ for g̃uahẽ)."
    ]
  },
  "grammar": {
    "intro": "A Guarani verb has the person in front and the marks of tense, aspect and mood after it. The unmarked form works for the present and the past; the future is always marked.",
